    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Polynesian settlers lived where Honolulu stands by the 11th century, and the harbour, whose name means sheltered harbour, drew foreign merchant ships from 1794.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a> held court in the area in 1809 and 1810, and in 1845 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii">Kamehameha III</a> made Honolulu the permanent capital of the Hawaiian Kingdom.</li><li>The monarchy was overthrown in 1893, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> in 1898, and Honolulu became the capital of the territory and, from 1959, of the state.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Honolulu is Hawaiian for sheltered harbor or calm port, and its older name, Kou, covered the area that is now the heart of the downtown district.</p></aside>
